# .env.example  —  copy to .env, which .gitignore already excludes.
#
# SECRETS, plus the few settings that describe WHERE THIS PROCESS RUNS. Nothing else.
#
# (This file previously said "secrets only, there will never be a fifth entry". The
# relayer's exposure controls made that too narrow, so the rule is restated rather than
# quietly broken: a value belongs here if it is a secret, or if it describes this
# particular deployment. Network parameters still never do.)
#
# Network parameters — pool address, RPC hosts, chain ID, prover, explorer — are FACTS
# ABOUT A NETWORK, not secrets. They live in packages/protocol/src/constants.ts where
# they are version-controlled, reviewable and diffable. Putting an RPC URL or a pool
# address here instead would mean the value a judge can read in the repository is not
# provably the value the code ran against. Do not add one.
#
# THE DEPLOYER AND THE RELAYER MUST BE TWO SEPARATELY FUNDED WALLETS.
# This is not a convention, it is a test requirement. Task 8's identity_key probe proves
# that the pool derives a DIFFERENT per-user handle for different callers of the same
# helper contract. With one wallet playing both roles there are not two callers, so the
# probe cannot distinguish "scoped per user" from "constant", and it proves nothing.
# Fund both before deploying.

DEPLOYER_ADDRESS=0x...
DEPLOYER_PRIVATE_KEY=0x...
RELAYER_ADDRESS=0x...
RELAYER_PRIVATE_KEY=0x...


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Relayer exposure controls. All optional, and the defaults are the safe ones — but
# read this before putting the relayer behind anything.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED IF THIS SERVER IS REACHABLE THROUGH A PROXY. Not optional there.
#
# A shared secret, sent by the caller as the `x-relayer-auth` header. When set, every
# request must carry it; without it the server answers 401 and never touches the key.
#
# Why a proxy makes this mandatory: the browser posts to the same-origin relative path
# /api/submit, and the server accepts that path precisely so a proxy can forward it.
# The moment that rewrite exists, loopback has stopped being a boundary — and NOTHING
# warns you, because the off-host warning keys off RELAYER_HOST, which you never
# changed. Behind a proxy every internet client arrives without an Origin header, which
# is exactly the shape the other checks treat as a trusted same-process caller.
#
# Content-type is a CSRF control. It is not authentication. This is.
# Generate one with: openssl rand -hex 32
# RELAYER_AUTH_TOKEN=

# Browser origins allowed to post, comma-separated. Default (unset) is the safe one.
#
# Be clear about what this can do: it can REFUSE, and it cannot GRANT. A browser
# request carrying both an Origin and content-type: application/json is by definition
# cross-origin, so it is preflighted — and this server answers no CORS headers, so the
# request never arrives. Configuring an origin does not enable a browser caller; it
# only narrows which non-browser callers are refused. Do not reach for it as a way to
# let a web app in, and do not mistake it for RELAYER_AUTH_TOKEN.
#
# Blank entries are dropped, so an empty value and a stray comma cannot widen it.
# RELAYER_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://app.example

# Interface to bind. Defaults to 127.0.0.1, and empty is treated as unset.
#
# Setting this exposes a funded signer beyond this machine. Treat it as a gate that
# authentication and rate limiting must come BEFORE — the allowlist bounds what may be
# signed, never by whom or how often.
# RELAYER_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Port. Defaults to 8787.
# PORT=8787
